From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:11:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: vdpu381-vp9: allow frames smaller than the
 CAPTURE format

VP9 can change resolution on inter frames, which then predict from
references of the previous size through the scaled-reference registers
the driver already programs. The driver however rejected every frame
whose 64-aligned size differs from the decoded format, so userspace had
to reallocate the CAPTURE queue, which frees exactly those references.
This breaks the vp90-2-21-resize_inter_* conformance vectors, which
shrink to 1/2 or 3/4 on a non-keyframe.

Accept frames that fit in the allocated CAPTURE buffers and decode them
in place. All CAPTURE buffers share the layout of the decoded format, so
take the reference luma size and the motion-vector offset from the
format instead of from the size of the frame stored in each buffer; the
per-reference frame size and scale registers keep using the real sizes.
Frames larger than the CAPTURE buffers are still rejected.

Userspace must also keep the queue across such a change: GStreamer's
v4l2slvp9dec currently drops frames on non-keyframe size changes.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 .../rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu381-vp9.c      | 19 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu381-vp9.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu381-vp9.c
index e90d0a8aa961..9b08e32fbdc9 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu381-vp9.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-vdpu381-vp9.c
@@ -281,7 +281,12 @@ static void config_ref_registers(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx,
 	struct rkvdec_vp9_ctx *vp9_ctx = ctx->priv;
 	struct rkvdec_vdpu381_regs_vp9 *regs = &vp9_ctx->regs;
 
-	aligned_height = round_up(ref_buf->vp9.height, 64);
+	/*
+	 * Every CAPTURE buffer has the layout of the decoded format, whatever
+	 * the size of the frame stored in it, so plane offsets come from the
+	 * format while the frame size registers use the frame's own size.
+	 */
+	aligned_height = round_up(ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.height, 64);
 
 	switch (i) {
 	case 0:
@@ -418,7 +423,7 @@ static dma_addr_t get_mv_base_addr(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx, struct rkvdec_decoded
 {
 	unsigned int aligned_pitch, aligned_height, yuv_len;
 
-	aligned_height = round_up(buf->vp9.height, 64);
+	aligned_height = round_up(ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.height, 64);
 
 	aligned_pitch = ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.plane_fmt[0].bytesperline;
 	yuv_len = (aligned_height * aligned_pitch * 3) / 2;
@@ -647,11 +652,13 @@ static int validate_dec_params(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx,
 	aligned_height = round_up(dec_params->frame_height_minus_1 + 1, 64);
 
 	/*
-	 * Userspace should update the capture/decoded format when the
-	 * resolution changes.
+	 * VP9 can change resolution on inter frames, which then predict from
+	 * references of the previous size. Frames that fit in the allocated
+	 * CAPTURE buffers are decoded in place so that those references stay
+	 * available; userspace must reallocate only when a frame is larger.
 	 */
-	if (aligned_width != ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.width ||
-	    aligned_height != ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.height) {
+	if (aligned_width > ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.width ||
+	    aligned_height > ctx->decoded_fmt.fmt.pix_mp.height) {
 		dev_err(ctx->core->dev,
 			"unexpected bitstream resolution %dx%d\n",
 			dec_params->frame_width_minus_1 + 1,
